meta {
  name: Lead Pull - Start Run
  type: http
  seq: 18
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/meta/lead-pull/runs?tenant_id={{tenantId}}
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Cookie: auth_token={{authToken}}
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "org_ids": [],
    "page_ids": [],
    "campaign_ids": [],
    "since": "2026-07-28"
  }
}

docs {
  Starts a pull: the backfill for leads the live webhook missed (an integration
  that was down, a page mapped late, a form nobody knew about). Replaces the
  three-stage Python CLI in msq-lms/meta-sync-scripts.

  Answers 202 with { run_id } from ONE FAST TRANSACTION. The pull itself takes
  minutes and is run by a poller inside meta-conversion-api, so nothing rides on
  the gateway timeout — poll Lead-Pull-Get-Run.bru for progress.

  SCOPE CONTRACT. Three of these default to "all":
    tenant_id     REQUIRED (?tenant_id=, never the caller's own)
    org_ids[]     optional, empty = every org in the tenant
    page_ids[]    optional, empty = every page mapped to those orgs (SERVER-SIDE)
    campaign_ids[] optional, empty = every campaign (applied POST-FETCH)
    since         REQUIRED, "2026-07-28" or a full ISO timestamp (post-fetch)
    until         optional, defaults to now (post-fetch)

  `since` is required even though download_page_leads.py defaults it: an
  unbounded pull across every page of a large tenant is exactly the operation
  that hits Meta throttling from a button press, and the person pressing it has
  no way to know that in advance.

  Starting a pull DELETES this tenant's previous run and, by cascade, its staged
  rows — nothing from the last pull survives into the next.

  Regression checks:
    * start a run, then start another while it is queued/running/applying -> 409,
      and the LIVE RUN MUST STILL EXIST afterwards (the 409 check happens before
      the DELETE, so a second click cannot delete a run out from under the worker)
    * start a second run after the first completed -> 202, and the first run's
      rows are gone
    * since omitted, or unparseable          -> 422
    * until earlier than since               -> 422
    * a page_id that is not 17-or-fewer digits -> 422 (Meta ids are STRINGS; a JS
      number would corrupt the low digits and pull the wrong page)
    * a bogus tenant_id                      -> 404 "Tenant not found"
    * caller below super_admin               -> 403
    * KILL THE SERVICE MID-RUN: the run must land `failed` via the reaper within
      META_LEAD_PULL_STALE_MINUTES, not sit in `running` forever — otherwise the
      409 guard above locks that tenant out permanently. Look for
      evt: lead_pull.runs_reaped on restart.
}
